-- Issue #893 case 3: a decision inside WHILE shipped with no false flow.
--
-- `while $C begin if X then break; end if; end while;` built an ExclusiveSplit
-- carrying ONLY its `true` outgoing flow (to the BreakEvent). The `false` case
-- was deferred to a following statement that never came, so the decision was
-- written with one edge — CE0079 "the 'false' condition value should be
-- configured on an outgoing sequence flow".
--
-- The identical body under LOOP was already correct. addLoopStatement had
-- tracked the deferred case and wired the leftover to a synthesized
-- ContinueEvent since ledger #52; addWhileStatement mirrored neither half, so
-- the two loop builders disagreed on the same body. Measured before the fix:
--
--     LOOP  -> split true=1 false=1
--     WHILE -> split true=1 false=0
--
-- Four WHILE shapes were affected, all of them a merge-less split:
-- `if X then break` last, `if X then continue` last, either with a trailing
-- statement, and either after a leading statement.
--
-- NOT WHAT THE ISSUE REPORTED. #893 case 3 claimed `IF` as the FIRST statement
-- of a WHILE body was written broken. That shape builds correctly on main and
-- did not reproduce; the defect above was found by probing its neighbourhood.
-- Cases 1, 2 and 6 of the same issue were already fixed by MDL061/062/063.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE CANNOT BE THE REGRESSION TEST
--
-- This is a WRITER defect, not a check gap. The MDL below is valid and `mxcli
-- check` is right to accept it — before the fix it reported "Syntax OK" and
-- exit 0, and `exec` then wrote the broken microflow. So this file must be a
-- plain .mdl, never a .fail.mdl: `make check-mdl` runs `mxcli check` without a
-- project, and a negative test here would fail as "unexpectedly passed".
--
-- The guard is in mdl/executor/cmd_microflows_while_break_test.go, which
-- inspects the built graph directly:
--
--   TestBuilder_ConditionalBreakLastInWhile_HasFalseFlow
--   TestBuilder_MergelessSplitFalseFlow_LoopAndWhileAgree
--   TestBuilder_MergelessSplitMidWhileBody_FalseFlowReachesNextStatement
--
-- The parity test is the one that matters long-term: it asserts LOOP and WHILE
-- agree on the same body, which is what would have caught this defect when the
-- ledger #52 fix was applied to only one of the two builders.
--
-- To see the original symptom, revert the pendingCase block in
-- addWhileStatement (mdl/executor/cmd_microflows_builder_control.go) and re-run
-- those three tests.

CREATE OR MODIFY MICROFLOW MyModule.ZZ_WhileBreak ()
BEGIN
  DECLARE $Continue Boolean = true;
  WHILE $Continue
  BEGIN
    IF 1 = 1 THEN
      BREAK;
    END IF;
  END WHILE;
END;
/

-- The same shape with a trailing statement: the deferred false case must reach
-- the LOG rather than being dropped.
CREATE OR MODIFY MICROFLOW MyModule.ZZ_WhileBreakThenStatement ()
BEGIN
  DECLARE $Continue Boolean = true;
  WHILE $Continue
  BEGIN
    IF 1 = 1 THEN
      BREAK;
    END IF;
    LOG INFO NODE 'probe' 'after the split';
  END WHILE;
END;
/

-- CONTINUE is the other merge-less exit. (RETURN inside a loop is refused by
-- MDL062, so it is not a live shape here.)
CREATE OR MODIFY MICROFLOW MyModule.ZZ_WhileContinue ()
BEGIN
  DECLARE $Continue Boolean = true;
  WHILE $Continue
  BEGIN
    IF 1 = 1 THEN
      CONTINUE;
    END IF;
  END WHILE;
END;
/

-- The LOOP control: this shape was already correct, and must stay that way.
CREATE OR MODIFY MICROFLOW MyModule.ZZ_LoopBreakControl ()
BEGIN
  RETRIEVE $PartList FROM MyModule.Part;
  LOOP $Part IN $PartList
  BEGIN
    IF 1 = 1 THEN
      BREAK;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;
END;
/
